"""Source-based AI Scan API reference. No live tests were run."""
MODULES = {
  "api": "<article class=\"deep-dive-section\" id=\"sxf-ai-scan-api-examples\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">SXF VERIFIED REFERENCE</p><h2>GitHub AI Scan REST API Examples: GET and PATCH repository_scan</h2><p><strong>Documentation-derived, unexecuted examples.</strong> Replace ORG, OWNER and REPO; export an authorized GH_TOKEN. Requests use GitHub API version <code>2026-03-10</code>. A PATCH modifies configuration only and does not trigger a scan.</p><h3>Read organization setting</h3><pre style=\"white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto;padding:18px;background:#0b111b;border:1px solid #455469;border-radius:14px\"><code>curl -L -sS \\\n  -H \"Accept: application/vnd.github+json\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $GH_TOKEN\" \\\n  -H \"X-GitHub-Api-Version: 2026-03-10\" \\\n  https://api.github.com/orgs/ORG/code-scanning/ai-scan</code></pre><h3>Enable organization repository_scan</h3><pre style=\"white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto;padding:18px;background:#0b111b;border:1px solid #455469;border-radius:14px\"><code>curl -L -sS -X PATCH \\\n  -H \"Accept: application/vnd.github+json\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $GH_TOKEN\" \\\n  -H \"X-GitHub-Api-Version: 2026-03-10\" \\\n  https://api.github.com/orgs/ORG/code-scanning/ai-scan \\\n  -d '{\"repository_scan\":\"enabled\"}'</code></pre><h3>Read repository setting</h3><pre style=\"white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto;padding:18px;background:#0b111b;border:1px solid #455469;border-radius:14px\"><code>curl -L -sS \\\n  -H \"Accept: application/vnd.github+json\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $GH_TOKEN\" \\\n  -H \"X-GitHub-Api-Version: 2026-03-10\" \\\n  https://api.github.com/repos/OWNER/REPO/code-scanning/ai-scan</code></pre><h3>Disable one repository</h3><pre style=\"white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto;padding:18px;background:#0b111b;border:1px solid #455469;border-radius:14px\"><code>curl -L -sS -X PATCH \\\n  -H \"Accept: application/vnd.github+json\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $GH_TOKEN\" \\\n  -H \"X-GitHub-Api-Version: 2026-03-10\" \\\n  https://api.github.com/repos/OWNER/REPO/code-scanning/ai-scan \\\n  -d '{\"repository_scan\":\"disabled\"}'</code></pre><h3>HTTP 422 example (illustrative, not captured)</h3><pre style=\"white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto;padding:18px;background:#0b111b;border:1px solid #455469;border-radius:14px\"><code>HTTP/2 422\n{ \"message\": \"[read GitHub validation details here]\" }</code></pre><p>A blocked enterprise policy is one documented cause of 422; it is not the only possible validation error. <strong>Required permissions:</strong> org GET needs owner/security-manager access and fine-grained <code>Administration:read</code>; org PATCH requires <code>Administration:write</code>. Repository GET needs <code>Code scanning alerts:read</code>; PATCH needs <code>Administration:write</code> for the repository and admin access. Classic PAT scopes: org endpoints document <code>admin:org</code>, <code>repo</code> or <code>write:org</code> according to role; repo GET documents <code>security_events</code> (or public-only <code>public_repo</code>), while PATCH documents <code>repo</code> (or public-only <code>public_repo</code>). Confirm the exact endpoint, actor role and entitlement.</p><p><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/rest/code-scanning/code-scanning?apiVersion=2026-03-10\">Official GitHub AI Scan REST API reference</a>.</p></article>",
  "hierarchy": "<article class=\"deep-dive-section\" id=\"sxf-ai-scan-policy-hierarchy\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">SXF VERIFIED REFERENCE</p><h2>GitHub AI Scan Policy Hierarchy: Enterprise vs Organization vs Repository</h2><p>Enterprise policy is <strong>Not allowed by default</strong>. Permitting AI Scan at enterprise level does not itself enable scanning. Lower-level settings are constrained by higher levels.</p><div class=\"deep-dive-table-wrap\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Layer\"><table class=\"deep-dive-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Layer</th><th scope=\"col\">Authority</th><th scope=\"col\">Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Enterprise</th><td>Enterprise owners</td><td>Permits or prohibits enabling AI Scan</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Organization</th><td>Organization owners and security managers</td><td>repository_scan enables eligible repositories or prevents opt-in</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Repository</th><td>Repository administrators</td><td>Inherit organization setting, or opt out when allowed</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"deep-dive-facts\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"AI Scan flow: enterprise to organization to repository and then manual scan\"><div><strong>1 · Enterprise ↓</strong><p>Allowed? No → blocked</p></div><div><strong>2 · Organization ↓</strong><p>Enabled? No → blocked</p></div><div><strong>3 · Repository ↓</strong><p>Inherited? Opt-out → disabled</p></div><div><strong>4 · On-demand scan</strong><p>Separate writer action; credits apply</p></div></div><p>Allowed path: enterprise permits → organization enables → repository does not opt out → authorized user triggers a manual scan. A disabled organization cannot be bypassed through repository PATCH. If either <code>pr_scan</code> or <code>repository_scan</code> fails in a combined update, neither changes.</p><p><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/concepts/code-scanning/ai-scan-for-repositories\">Policy inheritance and eligibility documentation</a>.</p></article>",
  "errors": "<article class=\"deep-dive-section\" id=\"sxf-ai-scan-errors\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">SXF VERIFIED REFERENCE</p><h2>GitHub AI Scan REST API Errors: 422, 403 and Configuration Failures</h2><p>HTTP status alone does not prove a particular cause. Inspect the actual GitHub response body and security policy.</p><div class=\"deep-dive-table-wrap\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Status\"><table class=\"deep-dive-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Status</th><th scope=\"col\">Possible cause</th><th scope=\"col\">First check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">422</th><td>Enterprise denial, validation conflict or blocked combined PATCH</td><td>Review enterprise policy, organization setting and requested fields</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">403</th><td>Token/role restrictions, entitlement, archive or repository state</td><td>Inspect fine-grained permissions, role and eligibility</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">404</th><td>Bad owner/repo path or unavailable/inaccessible resource</td><td>Verify URL, resource visibility and access</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">400</th><td>Malformed HTTP or JSON at a general request-handling layer</td><td>Validate JSON and headers; not a listed AI Scan PATCH-specific status</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Scope note:</strong> GitHub explicitly documents 403, 404 and 422 for the AI Scan PATCH operations. HTTP 400 is a general troubleshooting possibility, <em>not a promised endpoint-specific response</em>. The documented 422 caused by enterprise denial is one case, not a universal error translation.</p><p><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/rest/code-scanning/code-scanning?apiVersion=2026-03-10\">Official response codes</a>.</p></article>",
  "comparison": "<article class=\"deep-dive-section\" id=\"sxf-ai-scan-tool-comparison\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">SXF VERIFIED REFERENCE</p><h2>GitHub AI Scan vs CodeQL vs Dependabot: What Does Each Tool Do?</h2><div class=\"deep-dive-table-wrap\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Tool\"><table class=\"deep-dive-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Tool</th><th scope=\"col\">Main job</th><th scope=\"col\">Best used for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">AI Scan</th><td>AI-assisted vulnerability search in the default branch</td><td>Additional on-demand code security analysis</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">CodeQL</th><td>Semantic code analysis and vulnerabilities</td><td>Repeatable static code checks</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Dependabot</th><td>Vulnerable dependencies and security updates</td><td>Supply-chain and package updates</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Secret scanning</th><td>Exposed credentials and tokens</td><td>Credential discovery, rotation and prevention</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>AI Scan for repositories runs independently of CodeQL and adds findings to the same code-scanning backlog. It does not exhaustively inspect every file; results may be inaccurate or duplicate CodeQL. Keep Dependabot and secret scanning for risks AI Scan does not replace. In this mode, scanning is manual, default-branch-only, and uses Code Security AI credits.</p><p><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/concepts/code-scanning/ai-scan-for-repositories\">AI Scan limitations</a> · <a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/how-tos/secure-your-supply-chain/secure-your-dependencies/configure-dependabot-alerts\">Dependabot</a> · <a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/secret-scanning/introduction/about-secret-scanning\">Secret scanning</a>.</p></article>",
  "lab": "<article class=\"deep-dive-section\" id=\"sxf-ai-scan-deployment-lab\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">SXF VERIFIED REFERENCE</p><h2>SXF AI Scan Deployment Lab: A 7-Step Repository Security Test</h2><p><strong>SXF-designed testing plan, not an executed test.</strong> Use only a repository where you have authorization. Preserve baseline settings and the rollback plan; never log real tokens.</p><ol class=\"deep-dive-checklist\"><li>Choose an eligible pilot repository and identify its default branch.</li><li>Ask an enterprise owner to verify enterprise policy.</li><li>GET organization repository_scan and pr_scan values.</li><li>GET repository settings and confirm inheritance or opt-out.</li><li>PATCH only the approved field; re-GET and preserve rollback data.</li><li>Manually start a separate on-demand scan as a repository writer; record status and Code Security AI credit use. PATCH itself does not scan.</li><li>Triage AI Scan alerts and compare them to CodeQL, Dependabot and secret scanning.</li></ol><div class=\"deep-dive-table-wrap\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Pilot test\"><table class=\"deep-dive-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Pilot test</th><th scope=\"col\">Expected record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Policy validation</th><td>Allowed enterprise/org policy and eligible repository</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">API configuration</th><td>Successful PATCH plus confirming GET</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Scan execution</th><td>On-demand scan start and completion recorded</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Credits</th><td>Measured account/budget impact, not assumed pricing</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Findings review</th><td>Review log, duplicates, false positives, remediation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Record repository, original settings, permission class (not token value), response status, scan timestamps, credits before/after, alert overlap and rollback outcome. These are <em>expected observations</em>, not SXF measurement results.</p><p><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/concepts/code-scanning/ai-scan-for-repositories\">Official scan controls and billing guidance</a>.</p></article>"
}
